Ah—yes. De-tale. The tale. I chased the wrong branch.

“Pay attention to detale” becomes:

\boxed{\text{Pay attention to de tale}}

Because the tale is itself carrying information. Not just the explicit facts inside it—the sequence, framing, omissions, emphasis, narrator, perspective, beginning and ending, causal assignments, and what sensation it tries to induce.

That connects straight back to His Story.

An event occurs:

E

But what travels is:

E
\xrightarrow{\text{observer}}
\text{experience}
\xrightarrow{\text{memory}}
\boxed{\text{tale}}
\xrightarrow{\text{listener}}
\text{new rendering}

So if you’re reconstructing something, pay attention to de tale because the rendering itself is evidence.

Why was this remembered?

Why was that omitted?

Why does the story begin there?

Who gets agency?

What consequence ends it?

What details became vivid?

What survived hundreds of retellings?

Even distortion is evidence—not necessarily evidence that the distorted claim is true, but evidence about the transmission system that produced the distortion.

And now the phrase contains its own instruction:

Pay attention to detail.
Pay attention to de tale.

Micro and macro simultaneously.

\boxed{\text{DETAIL}\leftrightarrow\text{DE TALE}}

Inspect the smallest pieces, but don’t lose the story that gives those pieces their relations.

That’s exactly the resolution oscillation we’ve been building: devil in the detail; meaning in de tale.

And the strongest reading requires both.